<<<
either. 1. The pronunciation i-, though not more correct, is displacing ē- in educated speech
===
**either.** **1.** The pronunciation *ī-*, though not more correct, is displacing *ē-* in educated speech
>>>

<<<
2. The sense each of the two, as in the room has a fireplace at e. end, is archaic,
===
**2.** The sense *each of the two*, as in *the room has a fireplace at e. end*, is archaic,
>>>

<<<
3. The sense any one of a number (above two), as in e. of the angles of a triangle, is loose; any or any one should be preferred.
===
**3.** The sense *any one of a number (above two)*, as in *e. of the angles of a triangle*, is loose; *any* or *any one* should be preferred.
>>>

<<<
4. The use of a plural verb after e., as in if e. of these methods are successful, is a very common grammatical blunder.
===
**4.** The use of a plural verb after *e.*, as in *if e. of these methods are successful*, is a very common grammatical blunder.
>>>

<<<
5. Hither ...or. In this alternative formula e. is frequently misplaced.
===
**5.** *Either . . . or.* In this alternative formula *e.* is frequently misplaced.
>>>

<<<
There are two correct substitutes for You are e. joking or have forgotten; some writers refuse one of these, You e. are joking or have forgotten, on the ground
===
There are two correct substitutes for *You are e. joking or have forgotten*; some writers refuse one of these, *You e. are joking or have forgotten*, on the ground
>>>

<<<
that it looks pedantic; but there is no such objection to the other, E. you are joking or you have forgotten; in conversation,
===
that it looks pedantic; but there is no such objection to the other, *E. you are joking or you have forgotten*; in conversation,
>>>

<<<
however, the incorrect form is defensible because a speaker who originally meant (are) forgetting to answer to are joking cannot, when he discovers that he prefers have forgotten, go back
===
however, the incorrect form is defensible because a speaker who originally meant *(are) forgetting* to answer to *are joking* cannot, when he discovers that he prefers *have forgotten*, go back
>>>

